T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{I}{mgd}}
\]
For a rod about one end,
\[
I=\frac{1}{3}mL^2,\qquad d=\frac{L}{2}
\]
so
\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{(1/3)mL^2}{mg(L/2)}}
=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2L}{3g}}
\]
With \(L=1.2\,\mathrm{m}\),
\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2(1.2)}{3(9.8)}}=1.80\,\mathrm{s}
\]

The angular frequency is
\[
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{mgd}{I}}
\]
so
\[
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{(1.2)(9.8)(0.30)}{0.45}}
=2.80\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}
\]

The angular frequency is
\[
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{mgd}{I}}
\]
\[
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{(0.80)(9.8)(0.15)}{0.12}}
=3.13\,\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}
\]
The period is
\[
T=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}=2.01\,\mathrm{s}
\]

Start from
\[
T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{I}{mgd}}
\]
Rearrange:
\[
g=\frac{4\pi^2I}{m d T^2}
\]
Substitute:
\[
g=\frac{4\pi^2(0.64)}{(2.0)(0.25)(2.6)^2}
=7.48\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}
\]

For a uniform rod pivoted about one end,
\[
T_{\mathrm{rod}}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2L}{3g}}
\]
For a simple pendulum of length \(L\),
\[
T_{\mathrm{simple}}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}
\]
The ratio is
\[
\frac{T_{\mathrm{rod}}}{T_{\mathrm{simple}}}
=\sqrt{\frac{2L/(3g)}{L/g}}
=\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}
\]
The rod has a shorter period because its mass is distributed along the length, with center of gravity at \(L/2\), not concentrated at the end.
